WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:01.930
I'm Saurav.

2
00:00:01.950 --> 00:00:04.810
This is <b>D3Recovery</b> and it was developed

3
00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:08.950
at the X-ray and Emission Imaging Lab at Weill Cornell Medicine.

4
00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:11.558
This is our <b>Prism-PET 1</b> scanner.

5
00:00:11.578 --> 00:00:16.698
It has 40 detector blocks to detect the gamma ray emission from the human brain.

6
00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:23.206
Our PET detector block has a 16 × 16 crystal array and an 8 × 8 SiPM grid,

7
00:00:23.226 --> 00:00:26.982
with a 4-to-1 crystal-to-SiPM coupling ratio.

8
00:00:27.002 --> 00:00:32.758
This figure visually illustrates the first interaction in an inter-crystal scatter event,

9
00:00:32.778 --> 00:00:35.718
from here on also called ICS event.

10
00:00:35.738 --> 00:00:39.798
The 3D position of the first interaction in an ICS event,

11
00:00:39.818 --> 00:00:43.078
comprising crystal ID and the depth of interaction,

12
00:00:43.098 --> 00:00:46.070
are essential for PET scan reconstruction.

13
00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:50.410
Hence, identifying both accurately is the goal of this work.

14
00:00:50.458 --> 00:00:54.598
In a photoelectric event, as there is only one interaction site,

15
00:00:54.618 --> 00:00:58.662
it's relatively easy to identify the first interaction crystal.

16
00:00:58.682 --> 00:01:04.422
On the right, the observed SiPM pattern is our source or input to the framework.

17
00:01:04.442 --> 00:01:08.582
However, first interaction crystal identification is challenging

18
00:01:08.602 --> 00:01:13.446
in an ICS SiPM pattern due to multiple interaction sites.

19
00:01:13.466 --> 00:01:16.086
We found in our <b>Prism-PET 1</b> experiments

20
00:01:16.106 --> 00:01:21.286
that about <b>70 to 80%</b> events are these challenging ICS events.

21
00:01:21.306 --> 00:01:27.386
In the <b>511 keV</b> photon energy zone, where gamma radiation operates,

22
00:01:27.418 --> 00:01:32.422
Compton scattering is often observed, giving rise to more ICS events.

23
00:01:32.442 --> 00:01:38.246
Across crystals and depths, the photoelectric SiPM patterns vary significantly.

24
00:01:38.266 --> 00:01:42.726
Hence, a similar variation is also expected in ICS events.

25
00:01:42.746 --> 00:01:46.406
Using the center of gravity, or the CoG method,

26
00:01:46.426 --> 00:01:51.046
it is easy to locate the first interaction crystal in a photoelectric event.

27
00:01:51.066 --> 00:01:57.226
However, due to contamination, CoG fails significantly in an ICS event.

28
00:01:57.274 --> 00:02:00.294
LOR, shorthand for line of response,

29
00:02:00.314 --> 00:02:04.678
may also get affected due to mispositioning error in ICS events.

30
00:02:04.698 --> 00:02:10.182
Mispositioning errors accumulate per event and compromise the scan resolution.

31
00:02:10.202 --> 00:02:14.822
Our previous work, called <b>Recovery-Net</b>, uses a convolutional neural net

32
00:02:14.842 --> 00:02:21.702
with two branches for crystal ID and DOI prediction, with the ICS SiPM pattern as input.

33
00:02:21.722 --> 00:02:26.934
While it achieves decent performance, it only uses simulated data for training,

34
00:02:26.954 --> 00:02:30.534
lacking experimental SiPM patterns from the scanner.

35
00:02:30.554 --> 00:02:34.246
To further improve the first interaction position recovery,

36
00:02:34.266 --> 00:02:37.414
what if we first recover the SiPM pattern?

37
00:02:37.434 --> 00:02:41.894
By its nature, this task can be modeled as an image-to-image problem.

38
00:02:41.914 --> 00:02:46.054
Such a problem is involved, as it is not easy to perform recovery

39
00:02:46.074 --> 00:02:51.942
using conventional methods on an ICS pattern with overlapping interactions, as shown.

40
00:02:51.962 --> 00:02:57.766
This plot shows that majority of the ICS events have such overlapping interactions.

41
00:02:57.786 --> 00:03:01.894
Thus, we model the recovered distribution to be probabilistic,

42
00:03:01.914 --> 00:03:05.414
and we use a conditional diffusion model for this task.

43
00:03:05.434 --> 00:03:11.974
First interaction pattern and <b>10,240</b> total crystals are the two primary challenges

44
00:03:11.994 --> 00:03:14.054
in this data acquisition process.

45
00:03:14.074 --> 00:03:18.022
Since an ICS event consists of more than one interaction,

46
00:03:18.042 --> 00:03:23.046
it can be viewed as multiple photoelectric patterns summed with energy weights.

47
00:03:23.066 --> 00:03:25.766
For many crystals, a collimated source

48
00:03:25.786 --> 00:03:29.462
would have to be passed through every crystal to get such data.

49
00:03:29.482 --> 00:03:31.942
This is a time-consuming process.

50
00:03:31.962 --> 00:03:38.422
Thanks to our previous work, we have a photoelectric patterns dataset per crystal per DOI

51
00:03:38.442 --> 00:03:40.518
from a cylinder phantom experiment.

52
00:03:40.538 --> 00:03:44.998
We perform GATE simulation that yields crystal ID and DOI

53
00:03:45.018 --> 00:03:47.718
for every interaction of an ICS event.

54
00:03:47.738 --> 00:03:51.686
Using these, we find an experimental photoelectric pattern,

55
00:03:51.706 --> 00:03:54.310
which is weighted using the simulated energy.

56
00:03:54.330 --> 00:04:00.086
A weighted sum of these patterns results in a synthetic ICS SiPM pattern,

57
00:04:00.106 --> 00:04:02.214
which is the input to our framework.

58
00:04:02.234 --> 00:04:08.134
And the first interaction pattern, crystal ID and DOI are our target variables.

59
00:04:08.154 --> 00:04:11.894
We propose a novel framework called <b>D3Recovery</b>

60
00:04:11.914 --> 00:04:15.238
that stands for diffusion-driven tripartite recovery.

61
00:04:15.258 --> 00:04:19.798
Stage 1 is a conditional diffusion model that takes Gaussian noise,

62
00:04:19.818 --> 00:04:23.782
the ICS SiPM pattern and block ID embeddings,

63
00:04:23.802 --> 00:04:26.630
which encode the detector geometry, as input.

64
00:04:26.650 --> 00:04:30.310
Forward diffusion is carried out by adding incremental noise

65
00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:35.302
at each time step to the ground truth first interaction SiPM pattern.

66
00:04:35.322 --> 00:04:40.342
Then, the reverse diffusion process takes place by denoising the Gaussian noise,

67
00:04:40.362 --> 00:04:42.822
and a U-Net is used for this process.

68
00:04:42.842 --> 00:04:46.246
These are some results from this Stage 1 diffusion model.

69
00:04:46.266 --> 00:04:50.806
We benchmarked the CoG method using the recovered predicted patterns,

70
00:04:50.826 --> 00:04:53.542
showing significant improvement in CoG.

71
00:04:53.562 --> 00:04:57.478
However, diffusion is an image-to-image framework.

72
00:04:57.498 --> 00:04:59.654
Our goal is 3D position.

73
00:04:59.674 --> 00:05:03.462
Additionally, what if pattern recovery is incorrect?

74
00:05:03.482 --> 00:05:08.182
Stage 1 also lacks the ICS and first interaction correlation,

75
00:05:08.202 --> 00:05:10.838
as this may result in further improvement.

76
00:05:10.858 --> 00:05:14.918
Hence, we propose Stage 2 for accurate position recovery.

77
00:05:14.938 --> 00:05:21.862
Using the recovered and ICS SiPM patterns, along with the block ID embeddings, as input,

78
00:05:21.882 --> 00:05:25.558
we develop a physics-informed <b>CrystalAttentionNet</b>,

79
00:05:25.578 --> 00:05:31.782
comprising cross-attention and 16 × 16 spatial probability heat map prediction head,

80
00:05:31.802 --> 00:05:37.126
as opposed to a classification head in <b>Recovery-Net</b> for crystal ID prediction.

81
00:05:37.146 --> 00:05:41.494
We also have a guided depth model for DOI recovery,

82
00:05:41.514 --> 00:05:45.446
which uses crystal ID as a regularizer during training.

83
00:05:45.466 --> 00:05:50.278
These are event-level results with Stage 1 and Stage 2 predictions.

84
00:05:50.298 --> 00:05:52.038
In the highlighted case,

85
00:05:52.058 --> 00:05:57.078
we see that Stage 2 is accurate even when Stage 1 prediction is incorrect,

86
00:05:57.098 --> 00:06:00.038
due to including both patterns in the input.

87
00:06:00.058 --> 00:06:02.758
We also show some rare failure cases,

88
00:06:02.778 --> 00:06:07.910
where Stage 1 is correct but Stage 2 is wrong, or both stages are wrong.

89
00:06:07.930 --> 00:06:13.110
At the event level, we evaluate <b>D3Recovery</b> with center of gravity,

90
00:06:13.130 --> 00:06:16.870
<b>Recovery-Net</b>, center of gravity with Stage 1 baselines

91
00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:21.958
for crystal ID and DOI error width at full width half maximum.

92
00:06:21.978 --> 00:06:25.382
Crystal prediction distance is also another metric.

93
00:06:25.402 --> 00:06:28.934
In all the above, our framework performs the best.

94
00:06:28.954 --> 00:06:33.094
At the scan level, we can validate the improvement visually,

95
00:06:33.114 --> 00:06:38.630
particularly in the shown region, from a 3D ultra-Derenzo phantom experiment.

96
00:06:38.650 --> 00:06:41.222
A baseline comparison is presented.

97
00:06:41.242 --> 00:06:46.742
Quantitatively, we observe a <b>24%</b> peak-to-valley ratio improvement

98
00:06:46.762 --> 00:06:50.118
using <b>D3Recovery</b> for this phantom experiment.

99
00:06:50.138 --> 00:06:53.558
In this 3D Hoffman brain phantom experiment,

100
00:06:53.578 --> 00:06:57.798
we observe significant improvements in the certain regions as shown.

101
00:06:57.818 --> 00:07:00.838
We document the structural similarity index,

102
00:07:00.858 --> 00:07:05.094
mean absolute error and standard deviation for all the methods.

103
00:07:05.114 --> 00:07:10.646
In conclusion, we propose a practical experiment dataset generation method,

104
00:07:10.666 --> 00:07:16.166
a novel physics-informed <b>D3Recovery</b> framework that achieves superior performance

105
00:07:16.186 --> 00:07:21.830
in ICS recovery as compared to the analytical and CNN baseline methods.

106
00:07:21.850 --> 00:07:27.910
Our future work, called <b>D3RecoveryMux</b>, will focus on interleaved multiplexing

107
00:07:27.930 --> 00:07:32.870
using flow-matching diffusion model to further improve ICS recovery.

108
00:07:32.890 --> 00:07:36.660
Thanks to our principal investigator, Dr. Amir Goldan,

109
00:07:36.680 --> 00:07:39.960
Dr. Wanbin Tan, and all my team members.

110
00:07:39.980 --> 00:07:41.740
Thank you for watching.

